Online methods are typically built upon image-level instance segmentation models<|CIT-MASK|>.
Several works<|CIT-MASK|> utilize convolution-based instance segmentation models to segment each frame and associate instances by incorporating heuristic clues, such as mask-overlapping ratios and the similarity of appearance.
However, these hand-designed approaches always fail to tackle complicated cases, which typically include severe target occlusion, deformation and re-identification.
Recently, encouraged by the thriving of Transformer-based<|CIT-MASK|> architectures in object detection and segmentation<|CIT-MASK|>, a bunch of query-based online frameworks have been proposed<|CIT-MASK|>, which take advantage of the temporal consistency of query embeddings and associate instances by linking corresponding query embeddings frame by frame. These advances boost the performance of online VIS models, which become de-facto leading VIS performance on most benchmarks (especially on challenging ones such as OVIS<|CIT-MASK|>).

