Introduction
\label{SectionIntro}

Graph generation has become of great importance in recent years, with important applications in many fields, including drug design<|CIT-MASK|> and social network analysis<|CIT-MASK|>. However, compared with the huge success of generative models in natural language processing<|CIT-MASK|> and computer vision<|CIT-MASK|>, graph generation is faced with many difficulties due to the underlying non-Euclidean topological structures. Existing models fail to generate structures and features simultaneously<|CIT-MASK|>, or model arbitrary attributes<|CIT-MASK|>.

Moreover, while general purpose foundation models are built in NLP<|CIT-MASK|> which can solve all types of tasks through the generative framework, there are no such general purpose models for graph data, since current graph generative models cannot address regression or classification tasks. People even have to train separate models for different levels (node, edge, and graph) of graph learning tasks. Therefore, it is beneficial and necessary to build a graph model that can solve (a) tasks of all types, including generation, classification and regression; (b) tasks of all levels, including node, edge, and graph-level.

\paragraph{Our work.}To overcome the difficulties, we propose Latent Graph Diffusion, \textbf{the first graph generative framework that is capable of solving tasks of various types and levels}. Our main contributions are summarized as follows.

\begin{itemize}
\item We present Latent Graph Diffusion (LGD), which applies a score-based diffusion generative model in the latent space encoded through a powerful pretrained graph encoder, thus overcoming the difficulties brought by discrete graph structures and various feature categories.
\item Using a specially designed graph transformer, LGD is able to generate node, edge, and graph features simultaneously in one shot with all feature types. We also design a special cross-attention mechanism to enable controllable generation.
\item We conceptually formulate regression and classification tasks as conditional generation, and theoretically show that latent diffusion models can solve them with provable guarantees. This unified task formulation enables LGD to perform both generation and prediction tasks through generative modeling.
\item Experimentally, LGD achieves state-of-the-art or highly competitive results across various tasks, which verifies the effectiveness of our framework.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
