Introduction
Learning intrinsic low-dimensional representations from high-dimensional data without human supervision, i.e., unsupervised representation learning (URL), is a long-standing problem. As the high-dimensional data is usually highly redundant and non-Euclidean, a widespread assumption is that data lies on a low-dimensional ambient space. Given a finite set of samples $X=[x_1,...,x_n]\in \mathbb{R}^{n\times D}$, we seek a continuous mapping, $f_{\theta}(x):\mathbb{R}^{D}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{d}$, where $d \ll D$, that transfers data into a compact latent space $Z$ while preserving essential structures of $X$ to facilitate most downstream tasks. Generally, the intrinsic structures of data are determined by \textit{both} the input data and specific task assumptions.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{figures/Fig_introduction.pdf}
\caption{Illustration of various empirical structures of high-dimensional data. We map COIL-20, Wiki, and STL-10 test datasets to 2-dim, 128-dim, and 512-dim by GenURL (128-dim and 512-dim are visualized by UMAP<|CIT-MASK|>). \textbf{Left}: we preserve local geometric structures of the circle manifolds in COIL-20 in the DR task. \textbf{Middle}: the topological and geometric structures of citation networks in Wiki are encoded in the GE task. \textbf{Right}: with the instance discriminative proxy task, we learn a discriminative representation in STL-10.}
\label{fig:introduction}
\vspace{-12pt}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Dimension reduction and graph embedding.}
In many scenarios, we aim to encode non-Euclidean input data into a latent space $Z$ \textit{without any prior knowledge}, such as the clustering number or proxy tasks, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:introduction}. In the task of dimension reduction (DR), the manifold assumption is widely adopted, which assumes the data $x_i$ is sampled from a compact manifold $\mathcal{M}$ immersed in the high-dimensional ambient space $\mathbb{R}^{D}$. The intrinsic structure can be modeled by a distance of the input data, e.g., a Riemannian metric $g$ on $\mathcal{M}$ defined on the tangent space $\mathcal{T}_{x}\mathcal{M}$ for all $x\in\mathcal{M}$. To be more general, the input data is a graph $\mathcal{G}=(V, E, X)$ in the graph embedding task (GE), where $V$ is a vertex set and $E$ is an edge set. We optimize $z=f_{\theta}(x)$ that the Euclidean distance $d(z_i,z_j)$ can reflect intrinsic nonlinear relations, such as $<x_i,x_j>_{g}$, between the corresponding sample pairs. Inspired by DR, we propose a similarly-based framework that preserves the given input structures.

\paragraph{Self-supervised learning for discriminative representations.}
Complementary to DR and GR tasks, another popular form of URL assumpts the data has clustering structures and learns discriminative representations of clustering structures, as illustrated in Figure.~\ref{fig:introduction} right. It assumpts that the data $x$ is drawn from a mixture of $K$ distributions $\mathcal{D}=\{ \mathcal{D}_{k}\}_{k=1}^K$ and each component $\mathcal{D}_{k}$ is constrained to a relatively low-dimensional sub-manifold, say $\mathcal{M}_k$ with dimension $d_k\ll D$. And the data distribution $\mathcal{D}$ is supported by the combination of these sub-manifolds, $\mathcal{M} = \cup_{k=1}^K\mathcal{M}_k$, where each component can be somehow classified. Then, we can find a compact discriminative representation \textit{based on some prior knowledge}: two samples in the same low-dimensional cluster are invariant to a series of domain deformations or augmentations. For example, an object in an image is invariant to image augmentation policies like color jittering and randomly cropping.

Recently, self-supervised learning (SSL) approaches which learn discriminative representations by self-derived proxy tasks have largely reduced the performance gap between supervised methods, especially in computer vision. There are various of proxy tasks for visual presentations including handcrafted proxy tasks
